= Systems biology =

== Overview ==
'''Systems biology''' studies biological processes as interacting, dynamic systems by integrating experimental measurements with computational, mathematical, or network models.

Instead of isolating one component only, systems biology examines interactions among genes, proteins, metabolites, cells, tissues, organisms, and environments across scales. Feedback, regulation, network structure, and dynamic response are common concerns.

Systems biology is not defined by making a network diagram. Models can include differential equations, stochastic processes, constraint-based models, agent-based models, statistical networks, and multiscale approaches, each suited to different questions.

A systems model remains a scientific model: its boundary, parameters, observability, validation data, uncertainty, and perturbation tests determine what conclusions are justified.

== Boundaries ==
* systems biology ≠ network diagram
* systems biology ≠ systems science applied only metaphorically
* large biological dataset ≠ systems-level explanation

== Related concepts ==
[[Complex system]], [[Network]], [[Feedback]], [[System boundary]], [[Scientific model]], [[Genomics]], [[Bioinformatics]], and [[Adaptation (systems)]] provide the systems context.

== Model–experiment cycle ==
A useful systems-biology study specifies the biological question, measurement scale, model structure and quantities that experiments can constrain. Models generate predictions; measured responses to perturbations can then support or challenge the proposed mechanism. Repeating that cycle is more informative than fitting a network model to a single dataset.

== Dynamic feedback and regulatory control ==
[[Related to::Feedback|Feedback]] occurs when the consequences of a system's state or output influence later behavior. Feedback motifs appear in gene regulation, metabolism, signaling and physiology, but a causal feedback loop should not be asserted solely from a correlation network. Delays, nonlinearities and noise can make a feedback mechanism stabilizing in one parameter regime and oscillatory in another.

== Validation, identifiability and boundaries ==
A model may reproduce observed data while having poorly identified parameters or omitted interactions. Validation should distinguish fitting/training observations from independent perturbation tests, document units and assumptions, and compare alternative explanations. [[Related to::System boundary|System boundaries]] determine which cells, molecules, compartments or environmental inputs are included; that choice can change the model's predictions.

